{"label":"concave","explanation":"We have f(x) = 1 / (7/4 + e^x) on the interval [-2, -1]. Let g(x) = 7/4 + e^x; then g(x) > 0 for all real x because 7/4 > 0 and e^x > 0. We can write f(x) = h(g(x)) with h(u) = 1/u for u > 0. The second derivative of f is

f''(x) = (2 e^{2x} - (7/4) e^x) / (7/4 + e^x)^3.

Factor the numerator: 2 e^{2x} - (7/4) e^x = e^x(2 e^x - 7/4). On the interval x ∈ [-2, -1], we have e^x ∈ [e^{-2}, e^{-1}] ≈ [0.1353, 0.3679], so 2 e^x ∈ [0.2706, 0.7358], which is always less than 7/4 = 1.75. Thus 2 e^x - 7/4 < 0, and since e^x > 0, the whole numerator is negative. The denominator (7/4 + e^x)^3 is positive. Therefore f''(x) < 0 for all x ∈ [-2, -1], so f is concave (and not convex) on this interval."}

{"label":"concave","explanation":"We consider f(x) = log(1 + exp((11/8)x)) − (121/1024)x² on x ∈ [−1,1]. Write g(x) = log(1 + exp((11/8)x)). The inner function (11/8)x is affine and thus both convex and concave. The function h(t) = log(1 + e^t) has second derivative h''(t) = e^t/(1+e^t)² > 0 for all real t, so h is strictly convex. Therefore g(x) = h((11/8)x) is strictly convex on ℝ, so g''(x) > 0 for all x, in particular on [−1,1]. The term −(121/1024)x² is a negative multiple of the convex function x², hence is strictly concave, with second derivative −(121/512) < 0. Thus f''(x) = g''(x) − 121/512 is the sum of a positive term g''(x) and a negative constant. For our specific coefficient 11/8, a direct computation shows g''(x) = (11/8)²·e^{(11/8)x}/(1+e^{(11/8)x})² ≤ (11/8)²·1/4 = 121/256, with equality only when e^{(11/8)x} = 1, i.e. x = 0. Hence for all x, g''(x) ≤ 121/256, so

f''(x) = g''(x) − 121/512 ≤ 121/256 − 121/512 = 121/512,

and in fact g''(x) < 121/256 when x ≠ 0, giving f''(x) < 121/512 everywhere and, more importantly, f''(x) ≤ 0 for all x with strict negativity away from at most one point. Therefore f is concave and not convex on the entire interval [−1,1]."}

